From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 17:28:37 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] drm/virtio: pace the CRTC with a vblank timer

virtio-gpu has no vertical blank and the driver set up no vblank
support, so the atomic helpers completed every page flip as it was
queued: the flip event and the CRTC's out-fence signalled before the
device had the frame, at whatever rate the compositor committed.
Android's drm_hwcomposer sleeps until a quarter of a period before the
vblank it expects its commit to latch at and then commits, so it got a
present fence that signalled up to a period before the time it asked
for, which the CTS frame-timeline tests and VK_GOOGLE_display_timing
measure.

Pace the CRTC the way vkms does: initialise vblank handling for every
scanout and let the DRM helpers run a timer at the mode's refresh rate.
A flip event is armed for the next tick, and the timer runs only while
something holds a vblank reference, so an idle display costs nothing. A
client that flips faster than the refresh rate is now held to it, since
the next commit waits for the previous flip event.

The helpers started a timer that had stopped for want of a reference
one period after the enable, and the count and timestamp that the
enable stored were the time of the enable. Every restart after an idle
gap moved the grid of ticks, so a compositor that predicts its next
vblank from the last timestamp was off by up to a period after each
gap. Keep the grid across the gap instead:

 - a timer that starts again with the frame duration it last ran at
   expires first at its old expiry plus the fewest whole frames that
   put it in the future, whether that expiry fired or was cancelled
   pending;
 - the first start of a CRTC, a change of the frame duration, and a
   modeset or resume start a new grid. The last two pass through
   drm_crtc_vblank_off() and drm_crtc_vblank_on(), which store the
   present as the last vblank, so a count continuing the old grid would
   run backwards;
 - while the timer is armed, its timestamp for the vblank counter is
   the grid point before the next expiry, also in the enable that arms
   it. The core, which derives the count of a driver without a hardware
   counter from the timestamps, then adds the whole frames of the gap
   and stores a timestamp on the grid. A timer that is not armed stands
   for the present.

A vblank wait or a flip event after an idle gap thus reports a count
that advanced by the frames that elapsed and a timestamp on the grid it
had before, as a display that kept refreshing would.

The commit tail waits for the commit's own flip event rather than
counting a vblank, as the default tail does. The default wait warns when
it sees no vblank within 100 ms, which a loaded host can cause by
delaying the timer, and the flip event completes on the same tick
without the warning. No scanout needs either wait, because the host
copies the frame when the driver flushes the resource and nothing scans
out the old framebuffer afterwards. A commit must still complete on its
flip, though: without any wait a blocking commit, a SETCRTC for
instance, returns before its flip event, and the nonblocking page flip
that a client queues next is refused with -EBUSY for up to a tick.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/drm_vblank.c             | 60 +++++++++++++++++++++---
 drivers/gpu/drm/virtio/virtgpu_display.c | 54 +++++++++++++++++++++
 include/drm/drm_vblank.h                 |  7 +++
 3 files changed, 114 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_vblank.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_vblank.c
index 451ec96202266e7c345219d35b1c5983660f68aa..b4a172dee4431e1536f8fd07aaf0646dcc541489 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_vblank.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_vblank.c
@@ -247,6 +247,7 @@ static u32 __get_vblank_counter(struct drm_device *dev, unsigned int pipe)
  */
 static void drm_reset_vblank_timestamp(struct drm_device *dev, unsigned int pipe)
 {
+	struct drm_vblank_crtc *vblank = drm_vblank_crtc(dev, pipe);
 	u32 cur_vblank;
 	bool rc;
 	ktime_t t_vblank;
@@ -254,6 +255,12 @@ static void drm_reset_vblank_timestamp(struct drm_device *dev, unsigned int pipe
 
 	spin_lock(&dev->vblank_time_lock);
 
+	/*
+	 * The stored timestamp restarts here, at the present, so a vblank
+	 * timer that ran before no longer has a grid the count continues on.
+	 */
+	vblank->vblank_timer.grid_interval = 0;
+
 	/*
 	 * sample the current counter to avoid random jumps
 	 * when drm_vblank_enable() applies the diff
@@ -2216,8 +2223,18 @@ static enum hrtimer_restart drm_vblank_timer_function(struct hrtimer *timer)
  * @crtc: the CRTC
  *
  * Drivers should call this function from their CRTC's enable_vblank
- * function to start a vblank timer. The timer will fire after the duration
- * of a full frame. drm_crtc_vblank_cancel_timer() disables a running timer.
+ * function to start a vblank timer. drm_crtc_vblank_cancel_timer() disables a
+ * running timer.
+ *
+ * The first timer of a CRTC, and the first one after the CRTC's vblank was
+ * turned off and on again with drm_crtc_vblank_off() and drm_crtc_vblank_on()
+ * or after the frame duration changed, fires after the duration of a full
+ * frame. One that is started again after a cancellation with the same frame
+ * duration keeps the grid it ran on: it fires at the first of the instants it
+ * would have fired at had it never stopped that lies in the future, so the
+ * vblanks that follow are on the same grid as the ones before the stop, and
+ * the vblank counter, which the core derives from the timestamps for a driver
+ * without a hardware counter, advances by the whole frames that elapsed.
  *
  * Returns:
  * 0 on success, or a negative errno code otherwise.
@@ -2227,6 +2244,9 @@ int drm_crtc_vblank_start_timer(struct drm_crtc *crtc)
 	struct drm_vblank_crtc *vblank = drm_crtc_vblank_crtc(crtc);
 	struct drm_vblank_crtc_timer *vtimer = &vblank->vblank_timer;
 	unsigned long flags;
+	ktime_t interval;
+
+	assert_spin_locked(&crtc->dev->vblank_time_lock);
 
 	if (!vtimer->crtc) {
 		/*
@@ -2246,12 +2266,24 @@ int drm_crtc_vblank_start_timer(struct drm_crtc *crtc)
 	}
 
 	drm_calc_timestamping_constants(crtc, &crtc->mode);
+	interval = ns_to_ktime(vblank->framedur_ns);
 
 	spin_lock_irqsave(&vtimer->interval_lock, flags);
-	vtimer->interval = ns_to_ktime(vblank->framedur_ns);
+	vtimer->interval = interval;
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&vtimer->interval_lock, flags);
 
-	hrtimer_start(&vtimer->timer, vtimer->interval, HRTIMER_MODE_REL);
+	if (vtimer->grid_interval && vtimer->grid_interval == interval) {
+		/*
+		 * The expiry the timer last had lies on its grid, whether it
+		 * fired or was cancelled while pending. Move it on by whole
+		 * frames to the first one after now.
+		 */
+		hrtimer_forward_now(&vtimer->timer, interval);
+		hrtimer_start_expires(&vtimer->timer, HRTIMER_MODE_ABS);
+	} else {
+		hrtimer_start(&vtimer->timer, interval, HRTIMER_MODE_REL);
+	}
+	vtimer->grid_interval = interval;
 
 	return 0;
 }
@@ -2292,7 +2324,8 @@ EXPORT_SYMBOL(drm_crtc_vblank_cancel_timer);
  *
  * The helper drm_crtc_vblank_get_vblank_timeout() returns the next vblank
  * timestamp of the CRTC's vblank timer according to the timer's expiry
- * time.
+ * time. A timer that is not running has no vblank to report and stands for
+ * the present.
  */
 void drm_crtc_vblank_get_vblank_timeout(struct drm_crtc *crtc, ktime_t *vblank_time)
 {
@@ -2300,8 +2333,21 @@ void drm_crtc_vblank_get_vblank_timeout(struct drm_crtc *crtc, ktime_t *vblank_t
 	struct drm_vblank_crtc_timer *vtimer = &vblank->vblank_timer;
 	u64 cur_count;
 	ktime_t cur_time;
+	ktime_t interval;
+	unsigned long flags;
 
-	if (!READ_ONCE(vblank->enabled)) {
+	/*
+	 * The timer is armed before the core marks the vblank enabled and
+	 * counts the vblanks that passed while it was off. The vblank to count
+	 * then is the one before the timer's first expiry, which is on the
+	 * timer's grid, not the present: a count and a timestamp that stand
+	 * for an instant in between would not continue the grid.
+	 */
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&vtimer->interval_lock, flags);
+	interval = vtimer->interval;
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&vtimer->interval_lock, flags);
+
+	if (!interval) {
 		*vblank_time = ktime_get();
 		return;
 	}
@@ -2327,6 +2373,6 @@ void drm_crtc_vblank_get_vblank_timeout(struct drm_crtc *crtc, ktime_t *vblank_t
 	 * and what the vblank core expects. Therefore we need to always
 	 * correct the timestamp by one frame.
 	 */
-	*vblank_time = ktime_sub(*vblank_time, vtimer->interval);
+	*vblank_time = ktime_sub(*vblank_time, interval);
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL(drm_crtc_vblank_get_vblank_timeout);
diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/virtio/virtgpu_display.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/virtio/virtgpu_display.c
index e265a06f2c06ab2b9c79384346cfcf8278bffe3b..7c31ff32787d99f2b4df14bdbe3e9a199ba6f6f3 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/virtio/virtgpu_display.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/virtio/virtgpu_display.c
@@ -32,6 +32,8 @@
 #include <drm/drm_gem_framebuffer_helper.h>
 #include <drm/drm_probe_helper.h>
 #include <drm/drm_simple_kms_helper.h>
+#include <drm/drm_vblank.h>
+#include <drm/drm_vblank_helper.h>
 
 #include "virtgpu_drv.h"
 
@@ -55,6 +57,7 @@ static const struct drm_crtc_funcs virtio_gpu_crtc_funcs = {
 	.reset                  = drm_atomic_helper_crtc_reset,
 	.atomic_duplicate_state = drm_atomic_helper_crtc_duplicate_state,
 	.atomic_destroy_state   = drm_atomic_helper_crtc_destroy_state,
+	DRM_CRTC_VBLANK_TIMER_FUNCS,
 };
 
 static const struct drm_framebuffer_funcs virtio_gpu_fb_funcs = {
@@ -99,6 +102,7 @@ static void virtio_gpu_crtc_mode_set_nofb(struct drm_crtc *crtc)
 static void virtio_gpu_crtc_atomic_enable(struct drm_crtc *crtc,
 					  struct drm_atomic_state *state)
 {
+	drm_crtc_vblank_atomic_enable(crtc, state);
 }
 
 static void virtio_gpu_crtc_atomic_disable(struct drm_crtc *crtc,
@@ -110,6 +114,8 @@ static void virtio_gpu_crtc_atomic_disable(struct drm_crtc *crtc,
 
 	virtio_gpu_cmd_set_scanout(vgdev, output->index, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0);
 	virtio_gpu_notify(vgdev);
+
+	drm_crtc_vblank_atomic_disable(crtc, state);
 }
 
 static int virtio_gpu_crtc_atomic_check(struct drm_crtc *crtc,
@@ -133,6 +139,15 @@ static void virtio_gpu_crtc_atomic_flush(struct drm_crtc *crtc,
 	 */
 	if (drm_atomic_crtc_needs_modeset(crtc_state))
 		output->needs_modeset = true;
+
+	/*
+	 * The device has no vertical blank of its own, so the DRM core
+	 * paces the CRTC with a timer at the mode's refresh rate. A page
+	 * flip completes at the next tick rather than as soon as it is
+	 * queued, which is what a compositor that schedules frames against
+	 * the present fence assumes.
+	 */
+	drm_crtc_vblank_atomic_flush(crtc, state);
 }
 
 static const struct drm_crtc_helper_funcs virtio_gpu_crtc_helper_funcs = {
@@ -331,6 +346,37 @@ static const struct drm_mode_config_funcs virtio_gpu_mode_funcs = {
 	.atomic_commit = drm_atomic_helper_commit,
 };
 
+/*
+ * The default commit tail counts a vblank after it programs the planes and
+ * warns when it sees none within 100 ms. Here the vblank is a timer, which a
+ * loaded host can delay past that without anything being wrong. Wait for the
+ * commit's own flip event instead, which the same timer sends: a commit then
+ * still completes on the next tick, as it does on a device with a real
+ * vertical blank, so a client that queues a flip right after a blocking
+ * commit (a SETCRTC, say) is not refused with -EBUSY for the flip that is
+ * still pending.
+ *
+ * No scanout needs the wait: the host copies the frame when the driver
+ * flushes the resource, so nothing scans out the old framebuffer once the
+ * planes are programmed.
+ */
+static void virtio_gpu_commit_tail(struct drm_atomic_state *state)
+{
+	struct drm_device *dev = state->dev;
+
+	drm_atomic_helper_commit_modeset_disables(dev, state);
+	drm_atomic_helper_commit_planes(dev, state, 0);
+	drm_atomic_helper_commit_modeset_enables(dev, state);
+	drm_atomic_helper_fake_vblank(state);
+	drm_atomic_helper_commit_hw_done(state);
+	drm_atomic_helper_wait_for_flip_done(dev, state);
+	drm_atomic_helper_cleanup_planes(dev, state);
+}
+
+static const struct drm_mode_config_helper_funcs virtio_gpu_mode_helpers = {
+	.atomic_commit_tail = virtio_gpu_commit_tail,
+};
+
 int virtio_gpu_modeset_init(struct virtio_gpu_device *vgdev)
 {
 	int i, ret;
@@ -342,8 +388,16 @@ int virtio_gpu_modeset_init(struct virtio_gpu_device *vgdev)
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
+	ret = drm_vblank_init(vgdev->ddev, vgdev->num_scanouts);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	/* The timer starts and stops at no cost, so nothing keeps it running. */
+	vgdev->ddev->vblank_disable_immediate = true;
+
 	vgdev->ddev->mode_config.quirk_addfb_prefer_host_byte_order = true;
 	vgdev->ddev->mode_config.funcs = &virtio_gpu_mode_funcs;
+	vgdev->ddev->mode_config.helper_private = &virtio_gpu_mode_helpers;
 
 	/* modes will be validated against the framebuffer size */
 	vgdev->ddev->mode_config.min_width = XRES_MIN;
diff --git a/include/drm/drm_vblank.h b/include/drm/drm_vblank.h
index ffa564d79638494c5c51561422786763086d67ba..d643e5251a8ddffda9f0643a8752f9915d213a79 100644
--- a/include/drm/drm_vblank.h
+++ b/include/drm/drm_vblank.h
@@ -120,6 +120,13 @@ struct drm_vblank_crtc_timer {
 	 * @interval: Duration between two vblanks
 	 */
 	ktime_t interval;
+	/**
+	 * @grid_interval: Duration between two vblanks of the grid that the
+	 * timer's last expiry lies on, or zero when there is no such grid. A
+	 * timer that starts again with the same duration keeps the grid.
+	 * Protected by &drm_device.vblank_time_lock.
+	 */
+	ktime_t grid_interval;
 	/**
 	 * @crtc: The timer's CRTC
 	 */
